---
import KickoffSession from "../../components/KickoffSession.astro";

const photos = [
    {
        src: "/images/events/2026-kickoff/gallery/img_5363.jpg",
        alt: "Dr Sudarshana Bhattacharya speaking at the podium in a white blazer.",
        caption: "On wildfire exposure in Alberta's boreal forest.",
    },
];
---

<KickoffSession
    documentTitle="An area the size of one Nepal, every year. | Canada2080"
    description="Dr Sudarshana Bhattacharya on wildfire exposure in Alberta's boreal forest, from her cleaned remarks at the Canada2080 kickoff."
    eyebrow="Sovereign AI · 1:20 p.m."
    headline="An area the size of one Nepal, every year."
    name="Dr Sudarshana Bhattacharya"
    bio="Dr Sudarshana Bhattacharya is a senior director analyst and a recent graduate of Golden Gate University. Her doctoral research applies satellite data and machine learning to wildfire exposure in Alberta's boreal forest."
    profileUrl="https://www.linkedin.com/in/sudarshanabhattacharya/"
    podiumSrc="/images/events/2026-kickoff/gallery/img_5363.jpg"
    podiumAlt="Dr Sudarshana Bhattacharya speaking at the podium in a white blazer."
    photos={photos}
>
    <p class="lede">
        Every summer, she said, wildfire burns an area the size of one Nepal
        in Alberta's boreal forest.
    </p>
    <p>
        She had just graduated from Golden Gate University. At work she is a
        senior director analyst. She kept the talk on what Canada already has,
        and on one urgent problem that returns every year.
    </p>
    <p>
        Her numbers: 16.5 million hectares and $9.9 billion at stake each year.
        Indigenous communities who live there are most affected. The timber
        industry is hit hardest.
    </p>
    <p>
        AI, as she used the word, is not only ChatGPT. The field is about 70
        years old, and that older practice does not need the compute GPT models
        or Gemini need. Her research sits in the gap between satellite data
        and simpler machine learning, and a confirmed account of what is
        actually at risk.
    </p>
    <p>Two layers in the thesis architecture.</p>
    <ul>
        <li>
            Pull the data together and clean it. She said that foundation does
            not need the compute people assume.
        </li>
        <li>
            Start with Random Forest on an ordinary computer, then a
            convolutional network that tries to see the way an eye does, then
            an ANN that brings the parts together.
        </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
        The result she wants is a dollar figure for forest-fire exposure.
        Small, domain-specific language models could do it, drawing on Natural
        Resources Canada and Statistics Canada. Geospatial data is the
        remaining hard set. She has a project charter for a master's capstone,
        for anyone who wants to run it end to end inside a small language
        model.
    </p>
    <p>
        The doctorate, she said, was not the point. The point is a usable
        picture of what is at stake each year, built as much as possible on
        data held here. Some Copernicus satellite data from Europe may still
        be needed.
    </p>
    <Fragment slot="transcript">
        <p class="transcript-note">Cleaned up transcripts.</p>
        <div class="transcript-columns">
            <div>
                <h3>Keynote</h3>
        <blockquote>
            <p>
                Good afternoon, everyone. It's great to be here. My name is Dr
                Sudarshana. I just graduated from Golden Gate University, and
                at work, I'm a senior director analyst, as Francis mentioned.
            </p>
            <p>
                I'm keeping it very crisp and aligned to what Francis mentioned
                around Canada becoming stronger with what we have right now,
                pivoting on the materials that we have and at the same time
                trying to tackle the challenges that we are posed with every
                year. My research tried to address one of the urgent things
                that happens every year, focused on the boreal forest of
                Alberta.
            </p>
            <p>
                Geographically for context, every year we burn the area of one
                Nepal during our summer season because of wildfires. The people
                most impacted are our Indigenous communities who stay there.
                Industry-wise, the timber industry is hit the worst.
            </p>
            <p>
                On the other side, we hear a lot of things happening with AI.
                Let me keep it very simple here: when I say AI, it is not just
                ChatGPT. We have been doing AI for 70 years now, so let's start
                from there. That does not require the type of compute that GPT
                models or Gemini require. That is where the research
                intervention is. I will quickly go over some context to show
                you the numbers: 16.5 million hectares and $9.9 billion are at
                stake every year. The research tries to bridge the gap between
                satellite data, AI, machine learning, and probabilistic output
                on the left-hand side, and the right-hand side where we need a
                confirmed way to understand what is actually at risk. That is
                exactly the research intervention.
            </p>
            <p>
                This is a high-level architecture proposed as part of the
                thesis. It starts with the top layer where we pull data
                together. That is the foundation, and once we clean the data,
                that does not require the amount of compute we always thought
                it would. The second layer is the intelligence layer. We start
                with very simple, basic machine learning without going to any
                GPTs. We start with Random Forest, which can easily run on PC,
                and then move to CNN (convolutional neural network), which
                tries to replicate the way our eyes see and perceive. Then an
                ANN brings it all together.
            </p>
            <p>
                What it tries to solve is how we can account for the amount of
                exposure we have due to forest fires and arrive at calculated
                dollar figures. To implement that, it could be done using small
                language models, domain-specific language models, and most
                importantly, pulling together datasets from Natural Resources
                Canada and Statistics Canada. The only other challenging
                dataset we have is geospatial data. If we can figure out how
                best to pull geospatial data together, I would love to
                implement it as a capstone project. If anyone in a Master's
                program wants to implement it as a capstone project, I have a
                project charter outlining how to sequence these activities so
                we can see what end-to-end implementation within a small
                language model looks like.
            </p>
            <p>
                The idea here was not just to pursue a doctorate in AI or
                machine learning. The idea was to make it as applicable and
                sovereign as possible by leveraging in-house data. We may rely
                on some European satellite datasets coming from Copernicus, but
                that is all we would need to really implement it and have a
                clearer picture of what goes at stake every year and how we can
                protect it.
            </p>
            <p>
                I am happy to answer any questions you have and to continue the
                conversation after we wrap up. Thank you.
            </p>
        </blockquote>
            </div>
            <div>
                <h3 id="panel">Panel</h3>
                <p class="transcript-note">Francis Wang asked the questions.</p>
                <p class="ask">
                    What is the first distinctive thing we can do by
                    borrowing from patterns abroad? What are the patterns
                    we can accommodate in Canada?
                </p>
                <p class="transcript-note">Dr Sudarshana Bhattacharya.</p>
                <blockquote>
                    <p>
                        Another thing that I'm seeing happening a lot,
                        especially in the Asia-Pacific, is starting with not so
                        many billions of parameters. Start with a small
                        language model and go very domain-specific, because
                        there are certain domains where the data is cleaner. It
                        is always easier to start with those domains and train
                        your own model. Train it in your own backyard and get
                        it ready. In that way, you are not losing on compute.
                        You don't have to use anything that you have to procure
                        from outside. Small, domain-specific, homegrown models
                        would be one of the ways to think about it.
                    </p>
                </blockquote>
                <p class="ask">
                    What evidence should decision-makers demand before
                    calling an AI deployment here safe, secure, trusted, or
                    publicly beneficial?
                </p>
                <p class="transcript-note">Dr Sudarshana Bhattacharya.</p>
                <blockquote>
                    <p>
                        Governance, complemented with observability and
                        provenance, is also very important. I need to understand
                        why certain decisions were made and what really
                        triggered that kind of decision. If I go back to the
                        banking example, and a certain part of the community is
                        not getting a benefit, say a home loan or any type of
                        mortgage, why is that happening? Observability and a
                        clear decision provenance give that clarity, and that
                        runs in parallel with governance.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        Even when we do our best with whatever we deploy,
                        threat vectors expand every day. It is very hard to
                        stay a step ahead. This is where I see AI security
                        platforms as one way to keep watching for bad actors
                        trying to infringe a system.
                    </p>
                </blockquote>
            </div>
        </div>
    </Fragment>
</KickoffSession>
